# orchestrator.conf — copy to ~/.config/orchestrator.conf on the box and edit.
#
# Config for the G3 local-model orchestrator front door (scripts/orchestrator/
# orchestrate.sh). Parsed, never sourced. This file holds NO secrets — only a
# mode and a model registry (names, tiers, capability ranks, local endpoints).
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line; # starts a comment.

# Default routing mode. The CLI --mode flag overrides this per call.
#   AUTO        classify each prompt; sensitive -> local fleet, else best model
#   LOCAL-ONLY  force everything to the local fleet (the human seatbelt for
#               known-sensitive work — nothing can egress in this mode)
#   CLAUDE-ONLY human asserts the work is fine for the cloud (frontier eligible)
ORCH_MODE=AUTO

# The model registry. One ORCH_MODEL line per model:
#   ORCH_MODEL=<name>|<tier>|<rank>|<endpoint>
#     name      the model identifier (for a local endpoint, the Ollama model tag;
#               for the frontier tier, any label — Claude is invoked via its CLI)
#     tier      frontier | host-local | network-local
#                 frontier      = cloud, EGRESSES — eligible ONLY for non-sensitive
#                 host-local    = a model on your own machine, never egresses
#                 network-local = a model on another machine on the local net
#     rank      capability, higher = preferred within an eligible tier (integer)
#     endpoint  OpenAI/Ollama base URL for local tiers; omit for frontier
#
# THE INVARIANT: a sensitive task is never eligible for a frontier model, however
# high its rank — the router removes the cloud tier from the eligible set first.
#
# Examples (edit for your fleet; the endpoints below are illustrative):
ORCH_MODEL=claude|frontier|100|
ORCH_MODEL=richardyoung/qwen3-14b-abliterated:Q4_K_M|host-local|60|http://host.docker.internal:11434
# ORCH_MODEL=richardyoung/qwen3-14b-abliterated:Q4_K_M|network-local|55|http://10.0.0.21:11434

# --- Sensitivity classifier (AUTO mode) -------------------------------------
# The classifier is a LOCAL-only local-LLM judge. It is OPT-IN: until you point
# ORCH_CLASSIFIER at the script, AUTO stays fail-closed (everything treated
# sensitive -> local). These are read from the environment / .env, not parsed
# from this file — shown here for reference:
#
#   ORCH_CLASSIFIER=scripts/orchestrator/classify-sensitivity.sh   # enable it
#   ORCH_CLASSIFIER_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:11434     # LOCAL only
#   ORCH_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=richardyoung/qwen3-14b-abliterated:Q4_K_M
#   ORCH_CLASSIFIER_TIMEOUT=20        # seconds; a timeout fails closed (sensitive)
#   ORCH_CLASSIFIER_KEEP_ALIVE=30m    # keep the model warm between calls
#   ORCH_CLASSIFIER_PROMPT_FILE=~/.config/orchestrator/classifier-prompt.md
#
# The classifier refuses a non-local endpoint (it reads your raw prompt), disables
# qwen3 thinking, and asks for a one-word verdict at temperature 0. Tune what
# "sensitive" means by editing the prompt file (seed it from
# scripts/orchestrator/classifier-prompt.default.md); tuning stays on the local
# model, so it never leaks to the cloud. Measure it with
# scripts/orchestrator/eval-classifier.sh before you trust AUTO.
#
# PRIVACY: your tuned prompt file accumulates the sensitive patterns it is meant to
# catch. Keep it on the box (never commit it) AND guard it from Claude's own tools
# by adding its path to CTP_PII_PATHS in ~/.ctp-bridge.conf (the C1 path guard):
#     CTP_PII_PATHS=~/.config/orchestrator/classifier-prompt.md ...
# The classifier still reads it (it is not a Claude tool call); Claude cannot.

# --- Sanitiser (cloud-handoff path, control C2) -----------------------------
# Defense-in-depth on the NON-sensitive path: before a prompt is handed to Claude,
# rewrite it to strip incidental identifiers (names, emails, IPs, tokens, internal
# hostnames, codenames) with neutral placeholders, preserving the task. LOCAL-only
# LLM rephrase (it reads the raw prompt, so it never egresses). OPT-IN via
# ORCH_SANITISER; if unset, the prompt is handed off unchanged (the classifier is
# still the gate). Env / .env:
#
#   ORCH_SANITISER=scripts/orchestrator/sanitise.sh   # enable it
#   ORCH_SANITISER_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:11434   # LOCAL only
#   ORCH_SANITISER_MODEL=richardyoung/qwen3-14b-abliterated:Q4_K_M
#   ORCH_SANITISER_TIMEOUT=60
#   ORCH_SANITISER_KEEP_ALIVE=30m
#   ORCH_SANITISER_PROMPT_FILE=~/.config/orchestrator/sanitiser-prompt.md
#   ORCH_SANITISE_ON_FAIL=passthrough   # or 'block' — what to do if it can't sanitise
#
# passthrough (default): if the sanitiser fails, hand the ORIGINAL to Claude — the
# classifier already cleared it as non-sensitive. block: refuse the handoff.
#
# Same PRIVACY rule as the classifier: tune ~/.config/orchestrator/sanitiser-prompt.md
# (seed from scripts/orchestrator/sanitiser-prompt.default.md) — that directory is
# hidden from Claude's tools by default. Measure marker-stripping with
# scripts/orchestrator/eval-sanitiser.sh.

# --- deterministic term floor (#65) -----------------------------------------
# A private, owner-authored list of Org-sensitive terms, matched BY CODE before
# the classifier runs. A hit forces `sensitive` in every mode, including
# CLAUDE-ONLY. Missing or empty list = floor inactive (the classifier alone
# decides). Seed it from scripts/orchestrator/term-list.example.txt.
#
# The list is a distilled index of Org codenames, so it must stay out of Claude's
# reach — everything under ~/.config/orchestrator/ already is, automatically, and
# that cannot be switched off from a config file. These are env/.env knobs, not
# parsed from this file:
#   ORCH_TERM_LIST=~/.config/orchestrator/term-list.txt
#   ORCH_TERM_MIN_LEN=3

# --- local executor (#62) ----------------------------------------------------
# `orchestrate.sh --tools` dispatches a LOCAL-tier task to the executor
# (scripts/orchestrator/execute-local.sh) instead of a reasoning-only model call,
# so a sensitive task that needs file or shell work can actually be done here.
# Opt-in per run: without --tools the local path is unchanged.
#
# Every tool call is classified by scripts/lib/guard-stack.sh in executor mode —
# the same policy the PreToolUse hook enforces for Claude. An `ask` is confirmed
# on the controlling TTY; with no TTY it denies.
#
# ORCH_CALLER says who is on the other end and therefore what may be RETURNED:
# `human` (the default) gets the raw answer; anything else is cloud-bound and gets
# only a declared interface contract, after owner approval. These are env/.env
# knobs, not parsed from this file:
#   ORCH_EXEC_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:11434   # LOCAL only
#   ORCH_EXEC_MODEL=richardyoung/qwen3-14b-abliterated:Q4_K_M
#   ORCH_EXEC_MAX_STEPS=12        # bounds any single run, injected or not
#   ORCH_EXEC_MAX_BYTES=20000     # cap on tool output handed back to the model
#   ORCH_EXEC_CMD_TIMEOUT=120     # per-command wall clock
#   ORCH_EXEC_TIMEOUT=180         # per model call
#   ORCH_EXECUTOR_PROMPT_FILE=~/.config/orchestrator/executor-prompt.md
#
# Same PRIVACY rule again: the executor prompt is policy, not configuration. Seed
# it from scripts/orchestrator/executor-prompt.default.md, keep the real one on the
# box, and treat it as a one-way door — it does not go back to Claude to be
# reviewed, debugged or improved.

# --- disclosure boundary (#63) -----------------------------------------------
# What crosses to a cloud model is a DECLARED INTERFACE — name, invocation,
# inputs, outputs, exit codes — never the artifact the executor produced and never
# a scrubbed copy of it. Only named fields are copied, so undeclared content was
# never in the message; there is nothing for a filter to miss.
#
# A path is itself disclosure, so a contract refers to the artifact by an opaque
# handle. The handle-to-path map is a list of real internal paths and lives under
# ~/.config/orchestrator/, which Claude's tools cannot read:
#   ORCH_HANDLE_MAP=~/.config/orchestrator/handles.tsv
#
# The gates, in order: shape check (no paths) -> keep declared fields only ->
# term floor -> sanitiser -> term floor again -> owner approval. On THIS path a
# sanitiser failure BLOCKS (unlike ORCH_SANITISE_ON_FAIL on the prompt path).
# The owner may override that at the terminal, once, having seen the raw contract
# — there is no setting that leaves the override on, and it never skips the term
# floor.
#
# Approval needs a terminal, so Claude and the local model cannot co-operate
# unattended. That is a property of the design; see docs/ORCHESTRATOR.md.
